{"tool_name":"md-task","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"md-task","home":"https://github.com/RUBi-ZA/MD-TASK","summary":"Tool suite for analysing molecular dynamics trajectories using network analysis and PRS.","addition_date":"2018-06-11T18:49:44Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-25T14:39:46.164267Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:md-task","credit":[{"email":"o.tastanbishop@ru.ac.za","name":"\u00d6zlem Tastan Bishop","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://github.com/RUBi-ZA/MD-TASK"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Molecular dynamics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2476"}]}],"name":"MD-TASK","operating_system":["Linux","Mac"],"owner":"d.gabrielaitis","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btx349","metadata":{"abstract":"Summary: Molecular dynamics (MD) determines the physical motions of atoms of a biological macromolecule in a cell-like environment and is an important method in structural bioinformatics. Traditionally, measurements such as root mean square deviation, root mean square fluctuation, radius of gyration, and various energy measures have been used to analyze MD simulations. Here, we present MD-TASK, a novel software suite that employs graph theory techniques, perturbation response scanning, and dynamic cross-correlation to provide unique ways for analyzing MD trajectories.","authors":[{"name":"Amamuddy O.S."},{"name":"Atilgan A.R."},{"name":"Atilgan C."},{"name":"Bishop O.T."},{"name":"Brown D.K."},{"name":"Penkler D.L."},{"name":"Ross C."}],"citationCount":127,"date":"2017-09-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"MD-TASK: A software suite for analyzing molecular dynamics trajectories"},"pmcid":"PMC5860072","pmid":"28575169"}],"topic":[{"term":"Cheminformatics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_2258"},{"term":"Molecular dynamics","uri":"http://